{"id":"80092d98-88bc-4fe9-a7aa-cedff53bbf3a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785003099,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build id->node map and children list\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Precompute all descendants for each node (including itself)\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def get_descendants(nid):\\n        if nid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[nid]\\n        res = {nid}\\n        for cid in children[nid]:\\n            res.update(get_descendants(cid))\\n        descendants[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        get_descendants(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    removed = set()\\n    retained = {n['id'] for n in nodes}\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        sub = descendants[nid]\\n        # Check that no descendant (other than itself) is retained\\n        if (sub - {nid}) & retained:\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbols still present after removal\\n        remaining_ids = set(node_map.keys()) - removed - sub\\n        remaining_nodes = [node_map[i] for i in remaining_ids]\\n        remaining_symbols = {n['symbol'] for n in remaining_nodes}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            continue\\n        # Remove the entire subtree\\n        removed |= sub\\n        retained -= sub\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': sorted(removed),\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a payload object with `nodes` (list of node dicts) and `required_symbols` (list of strings). The task is to perform grammar-guided node reduction, similar to Perses reduction. The goal: remove removable nodes while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? But the schema output is just `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids`. The problem description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"interestingness oracle\". However, the fixture only has `nodes` and `required_symbols`. No grammar or interestingness function. Possibly the \"interestingness oracle\" is simply whether all required symbols are present after removal? Also \"grammar-valid\" might refer to the tree structure? Possibly we need to simulate a reduction algorithm that removes removable nodes in some order, respecting constraints: root is not removable, required symbols must remain (i.e., at least one node with that symbol must remain). Also nodes with children that are retained cannot be removed? The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained, then it must be retained.\n\nThe algorithm: traverse nodes in order of increasing depth (deeper first). For each node that is removable, attempt to remove it tentatively: check if all required symbols are still represented in the tree after removal (i.e., there exists at least one node with that symbol). Also check that the node's children are not retained (they would be removed or already considered). Since we process deeper first, children are considered before parents. So if we remove a child, then the parent's children set changes. But we need to decide removal order. The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes sorted by depth descending, ties broken by node ID (stable). For each node, if removable, we tentatively remove it, then check if the resulting tree still contains all required symbols. If yes, we mark it removed. But we also need to ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children. Since we process children first, if a child is retained (i.e., not removed), then the parent should not be removed. So we need to keep track of which nodes are retained. Initially all nodes are candidates for retention. When we remove a node, we exclude it from the set. But we must also ensure that if we remove a parent, its children (if still present) become orphans? The problem likely expects that removal of a node also removes its entire subtree. That's typical in tree reduction: removing a node removes the subtree rooted at that node. So we need to simulate that. The condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that if any descendant is retained, we cannot remove the ancestor. So we need to check that all descendants of the candidate node are either already removed or we plan to remove them (but we process depth-first, so children are processed first, so if a child is retained, we cannot remove parent). So algorithm:\n\n- Build tree structure: for each node, store children list.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending (high depth first), and for same depth, by id string (stable).\n- Initialize `removed` set empty, `retained` set all node ids initially.\n- Iterate over sorted node list:\n  - If node is not removable (removable=false) then skip (must be retained).\n  - If node.id is root? root has parent null, and removable=false, so it won't be here.\n  - Check: if any of its descendants are in `retained` set? But since we process deeper first, descendants have already been considered. So if a descendant was retained (not removed), then we cannot remove this node. So we need to check if the subtree rooted at this node has any retained nodes (other than itself? Actually, if we remove this node, all its descendants are removed too. So we should check if any descendant is retained. But retained set currently contains nodes that were not removed. So if any descendant is in retained set, we cannot remove this node.\n  - Additionally, check that after removing this node and its entire subtree, all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree (i.e., there exists at least one node with each required symbol among all nodes not in removed set (and not in the subtree of this node). Since we haven't removed yet, we can compute: for each required symbol, check if there is any node with that symbol that is not this node or any of its descendants. If yes, then removal is allowed.\n  - If both conditions satisfied, mark node as removed: add its id and all its descendant ids to removed set (but descendants might already be removed if we removed them earlier? But we process deeper first, so if a descendant was removable, we would have attempted to remove it earlier. If we removed it, then it's already in removed set. However, if we remove a descendant, then when we process the parent, the descendant will be in removed set, so it's fine. But we should ensure that we don't double-add. So we can just add the node id to removed set, and also remove all its descendants from retained set? Actually we maintain a retained set. When we remove a node, we need to remove its entire subtree from retained set. Since we process top-down depth-first, we can't easily remove descendants later because they were already processed. But we need consistency: if we decide to remove a parent, any descendants that were previously tentatively retained must become removed as well. However, the algorithm as described suggests we process deeper first, so children are considered before parents. If a child was removed, it's fine. If a child was retained (because it's not removable? or because we chose to keep it), then when we reach the parent, we should not remove the parent if any child is retained. That's the check we do. So we never remove a parent if any descendant is retained. Therefore, the only way a parent is removed is if all its descendants have already been removed (or are not retained). So when we remove a parent, all its descendants are either already removed or will be removed (since we are removing them now). But we need to update retained set to exclude descendants that were previously retained. But if we processed descendants first and decided to retain them, then we will fail the condition and not remove the parent. So no conflict.\n\nBut what about a scenario where a descendant is removable but we choose to keep it because removing it would cause loss of required symbols? Then the parent cannot be removed either. That's correct.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Build mapping id->node, and children list.\n- Determine root (id with parent null).\n- Required symbols set.\n- Retained set initially all node ids.\n- Sorted nodes: sort by depth descending, then id ascending (string).\n- For each node in sorted list:\n  - if not node['removable']: continue\n  - check if any of its descendants (including itself) are in retained set? Actually we need to check if any descendant (child, grandchild, etc.) is retained. But we also need to consider that if the node itself is retained (which it is currently), we want to remove it, so we are considering removing it. The condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means if any child (direct child) is retained, we cannot remove. But also if a descendant deeper is retained? The phrase \"retained children\" might be ambiguous: it could mean children that are retained (i.e., not removed). But if a child is removed, but a grandchild is retained, that would be impossible because removing a child removes its entire subtree. So only direct children matter? The problem says \"a node with retained children\". I think it means any descendant that is retained. To be safe, we'll check all descendants.\n\nWe need a function to get all descendant ids of a node (including itself? for removal we need to remove itself and descendants). Use recursion.\n\nBut we need to ensure we don't check descendants that are already removed (since they are not in retained set). So we can compute: subtree_nodes = all nodes in subtree of this node (including itself). Then if any of subtree_nodes (excluding itself) is in retained set, then cannot remove? Actually if itself is in retained set, that's okay because we are considering removing it. But if any other node in subtree is in retained set, then we cannot remove this node. So condition: (set(subtree_nodes) - {node.id}) intersect retained set must be empty.\n\nIf condition holds, then check required symbols: compute all node ids that are not in removed set (and not in subtree of this node? Actually if we remove, those nodes will be removed, so we need to ensure that after removal, each required symbol is present among nodes that are not removed and not in subtree of this node. Equivalent: for each required symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol that is not this node and not in its subtree. We can compute: remaining_nodes_ids = all node ids - removed_set - set(subtree_nodes). Then check if for each symbol, any node with that symbol is in remaining_nodes_ids.\n\nIf both conditions satisfied, then we remove the node: add all subtree node ids to removed set, and remove them from retained set (they are no longer retained). Then we continue.\n\nAfter processing all nodes, we have removed set and retained set (which should be all node ids minus removed). Then output sorted lists: removed_node_ids sorted ascending (string), retained_node_ids sorted ascending.\n\nBut note that order of removal is not part of output, just the sets. The output contract expects `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids` as arrays of strings. Sorting